# charon integration step 54 after 546078ea5d: commit 5a716176a006c2234cde9fe2b45ef55e5b39f3f6
# first parent d5ba232f0bba63ad7e6a898976399cf8dadf81a6
# series charon: patch -p1 in charon/ of the upstream source
#
# charon: clauses about the associated types of earlier clauses keep their lifetimes in signatures
#
# e0a15d7 fills the erased lifetimes of the trait references of an item reference from the clauses
# they prove, instantiated with the arguments of the reference, and matches the header of the impl a
# reference names against that instantiated clause. A clause about an associated type of an earlier
# clause never matched: `Flatten<I>` requires `I: Iterator` and `<I as Iterator>::Item: IntoIterator`,
# and for the return type `Map<Flatten<option::IntoIter<&'a Vec<B>>>, F>` of
# `chelis_types::env::Env::lookup_constructors` (env.rs:324) the second clause instantiates to
# `IntoIterator<<{impl Iterator for option::IntoIter<A>}<&'a Vec<B>> as Iterator>::Item>`, which does
# not match the header `IntoIterator for &'a Vec<T, A>`. The reference
# `{impl IntoIterator for &'a Vec<T, A>}<'_, B, Global>` kept its erased lifetime, and so did the
# lifted associated types computed from it: `Flatten<.., &'a Vec<B>, &'_ B, Iter<'_, B>>`, which Aeneas
# rejects ("Expected a runtime region type").
#
# PristineClauses now also snapshots the associated types of the impls (those without parameters of
# their own), and normalizes the instantiated clauses before matching: an associated type of an impl
# reference (`TraitType` on a `TraitImpl`) becomes the value the impl gives it, instantiated with the
# impl arguments (post-order, at most 32 nested replacements). This applies to the clauses of the item
# reference and, recursively, to those of the impls it names (`Seq for Flatten<I>` behind
# `Peek<Flatten<..>>` in the test).
#
# Test: ui/lift-assoc-types-projection-clause-regions (new; `&'_ u32` and `Iter<'_, u32>` before,
# `'a` now).

